{"package":"xProgress","version":"1.1.1","assessment":"complete","packageSha256":"490122b53a91fd6c1639a9ae0f504d9e2e0dc6526681709e2a6ac42778ea0e22","scannedAt":"2026-10-24T12:03:22.810Z","scannerVersion":"0.1.0","rulesetVersion":"0.1.0","tools":{"powerShell":"7.5.12","psscriptAnalyzer":"1.25.0","psscriptAnalyzerExpected":"1.25.0"},"packageUrl":"https://www.powershellgallery.com/packages/xProgress/1.1.1","metadata":{"title":"xProgress","description":"xProgress makes progress bars in PowerShell simple: throttled Write-Progress calls, accurate percent-complete and time-remaining, and nested parent/child progress bars, all handled automatically instead of hand-rolled counter/timer/Id bookkeeping in every script. It also mirrors Write-Progress calls made inside a background job's scriptblock into your own session -- including nested activities -- via Write-xJobProgress, something no other PowerShell module does. Full stopwatch lifecycle control (Start-xProgress, Suspend-xProgress, Resume-xProgress) lets you exclude wait time such as API calls or sleeps from elapsed and time-remaining calculations.","authors":"Mike Campbell (thatexactmike)","owners":"exactmike","projectUrl":"https://github.com/themodulecollective/xProgress","publishedAt":"2026-09-07T21:18:16.793","updatedAt":"2026-10-24T09:20:01.213","tags":["Progress","Write-Progress","ProgressBar","Jobs","BackgroundJobs","Stopwatch","Performance","PSModule","PSFunction_New-xProgress","PSCommand_New-xProgress","PSFunction_Write-xProgress","PSCommand_Write-xProgress","PSFunction_Complete-xProgress","PSCommand_Complete-xProgress","PSFunction_Get-xProgress","PSCommand_Get-xProgress","PSFunction_Set-xProgress","PSCommand_Set-xProgress","PSFunction_Start-xProgress","PSCommand_Start-xProgress","PSFunction_Suspend-xProgress","PSCommand_Suspend-xProgress","PSFunction_Resume-xProgress","PSCommand_Resume-xProgress","PSFunction_Write-xJobProgress","PSCommand_Write-xJobProgress","PSIncludes_Function"]},"dependencies":[],"fileInvento